WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.570
Find your hours: count your week and run a time audit.

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.480
This is the video for Module 1, Understanding Your Time.

3
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:15.920
Every week has exactly 168 hours. That is 7 days of 24 hours. Nobody gets more.

4
00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.480
What differs from one woman to another is how much of that time is already spoken for.

5
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:32.840
Fixed time is time you cannot easily move. Sleep, your job or shop hours, the school run, cooking the main meal, worship services you attend every week.

6
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:43.220
Flexible time is everything else. For most women it is larger than it feels, because it leaks away in small pieces of 10 and 20 minutes.

7
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:53.920
And energy matters. An hour at 6 am after good sleep is not the same as an hour at 10 pm. Use your best hours for thinking work.

8
00:00:55.170 --> 00:01:03.340
For example, Ngozi runs a small provisions shop in Enugu and has two children. She writes down her fixed hours with a pencil.

9
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:10.280
Most adults need 7 or more hours of sleep, so 7 hours times 7 nights is 49 hours.

10
00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:16.720
Her shop is open 9 hours a day, 6 days a week. That is 54 hours.

11
00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:24.050
The school run and market trips take about 1.5 hours a day for 6 days, so 9 hours.

12
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:33.020
Cooking, washing up and laundry take about 2.5 hours a day. Over 7 days that is 17.5 hours.

13
00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.390
Church on Sunday and the midweek service come to about 5 hours.

14
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:51.520
Her fixed time adds up to 134.5 hours. Take that from 168 and she has 33.5 flexible hours. She had believed she had none.

15
00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:59.850
Memory is not reliable, so a time audit means writing down what you actually did, as you go, for one week.

16
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.550
Step one. Choose a normal week, not a festive season.

17
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:11.030
Step two. Make a log on a notebook page or phone, in 30-minute rows from waking to sleeping.

18
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.910
Step three. Every two or three hours, stop and fill in what you did in each block.

19
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:25.950
Step four. Be honest. Write phone, TikTok, or chatting with neighbour, if that is what happened. Nobody else needs to see it.

20
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:39.320
Step five. At the end of each day, mark each block with a letter. W for work, H for home, C for care, T for travel and waiting, P for phone, R for rest, and S for social.

21
00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:45.660
The key rule is to fill it in as you go. Waiting until night means you forget the small leaks.

22
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:53.690
After seven days, count the blocks for each letter and divide by two to get hours. Then compare with your guess.

23
00:02:54.040 --> 00:03:00.510
For example, Halima, a teacher in Kaduna who bakes cakes, guessed about 1 hour a day on her phone.

24
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:12.790
Her log showed 46 blocks of phone time. 46 divided by 2 is 23 hours, about 3.3 hours a day. The gap is 16 hours.

25
00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:19.240
She made one change: no social media before her baking orders are done, using an app timer.

26
00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:30.940
Cutting two or three of the biggest leaks can free several hours a week. The phone is usually first: phone time is often two to three times higher than people guess.

27
00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:40.280
Searching for things is another. Give every item a fixed home, like a hook for keys and a box file for receipts and school papers.

28
00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:48.100
Small market trips for one missing item cost a lot of travel time. A weekly list fixes much of this.

29
00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:53.270
For example, Funke in Ibadan did all three and won back roughly 2 hours a day.

30
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:59.680
Sometimes the problem is energy, not time. Tick these each evening for a week.

31
00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:10.870
If you feel exhausted most days even after rest, or low for weeks, see a doctor or health worker. Ongoing tiredness can have medical causes, such as anaemia.

32
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:26.480
To remember. Count your fixed hours to find your flexible ones. Log your real week. Fix the biggest leaks first. Do thinking work in your best-energy hours. And start small, with two habits and a 15-minute weekly review.

33
00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:34.620
Now it is your turn. Count your week, run your seven-day audit, fix three leaks, and keep two habits for two weeks.

34
00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:41.240
The audit is information, not a judgement, so be kind to yourself. Continue with the lessons below.
